ics • 306\nPowerful generation capabilities • 306\nAdaptive and optimization capabilities • 307\nCross-language support • 307\nExploring common code LLMs ���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� 308\nCodex • 308\nAlphaCode • 309\nStarCoder 2 • 310\nCode Llama • 310\nDeepSeek Coder • 311\nMapping downstream code tasks � ������������������������������������������������������������������������������������ 312\nText-to-code tasks • 313\nCode-to-code tasks • 314\nCode-to-text tasks • 315\nCode-to-pattern tasks • 316\nText-to-text tasks • 316\nTracing the evolution of code generation and completion ����������������������������������������������� 317\nCommon code generation and completion techniques • 318\nCode generation and completion based on visual orchestration • 320\nCode generation and completion based on input–output examples • 322\nCode generation and completion based on code corpora • 324\nCode generation and completion based on functional descriptions • 327\nCode generation and completion based on language models • 329\nTable of Contents xviii\nIdentifying pain points in code generation and completion �������������������������������������������� 332\nUneven quality of training corpora • 332\nWeak generalization ability of program code • 333\nInformation asymmetry between functional descriptions and program code • 333\nApplying agent-based methods to project-level code generation ������������������������������������ 334\nPain points of project-level code generation in enterprises • 334\nDifficulty in fine-grained code modification and expansion • 334\nComplex and large codebases • 334\nDifficulty ensuring code generation quality • 335\nLow efficiency in implementing requirements into code • 335\nTechnical implementation of agents • 335\nCode agent testing process • 335\nTechnical implementation of code agent • 338\nApplication of transaction automation in development scenarios • 339\nProject R&D Q&A scenarios • 340\nFrom requirements to complete project-level code generation scenarios • 341\nSummary ������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������ 343","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":18,"pageEnd":19,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::13","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 10: Applying LLMs in Project Management 345","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 10: Applying LLMs in Project Management 345\nAnalyzing requirements and planning project tasks ������������������������������������������������������� 346\nRequirements analysis • 346\nThe value proposition of LLMs for requirements analysis • 350\nTask planning • 351\nImproving communication and collaboration ���������������������������������������������������������������� 352\nThe importance of communication and collaboration • 353\nLLMs as a solution to communication and collaboration challenges • 354\nThe role of LLMs in communication and collaboration scenarios • 354\nIntegrating and managing project knowledge • 355\nSharing information through meetings and screen recordings • 356\nConnecting communication platforms and project tools • 358\nLLMs improve communication efficiency and resource integration capability • 359\nTable of Contents xix\nImproving communication efficiency between different roles • 359\nSupporting decisions with historical project data • 360\nGenerating communication scripts for external stakeholders • 361\nManaging project risks and supporting decisions ����������������������������������������������������������� 362\nRisk management • 363\nPredicting bottlenecks • 364\nIdentifying risk points • 364\nDetecting abnormal behavior patterns • 364\nDecision support • 365\nMaking data-driven decisions • 366\nAllocating tasks based on team capacity • 367\nPrioritizing tasks and feedback • 368\nOptimizing decisions with real-time project data • 368\nPersonalizing decision support and risk warnings • 368\nOptimizing project execution with intelligent workflows ���������������������������������������������� 369\nChallenges in workflow and resource management • 369\nHow LLMs empower workflow and resource management • 370\nMaximizing resource utilization • 370\nOptimizing workflows • 371\nProviding practical advice • 371\nAutomation and AI generation • 371\nInformation identification • 372\nApplying LLMs in project management case studies ������������������������������������������������������� 373\nAssistance in understanding customer requirements • 373\nBackground • 373\nSolution • 374\nPractical results • 374\nImproving internal information circulation efficiency • 376\nBackground • 376\nSolution • 376\nPractical effects • 376\nTable of Contents xx\nImplementing automated project risk and progress analysis functions • 378\nBackground • 378\nSolution • 378\nPractical outcomes • 378\nEfficient and rational support for task allocation • 380\nBackground • 380\nSolution • 380\nPractical results • 381\nSummary ������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������ 382","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":19,"pageEnd":21,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::14","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 11: Applying LLMs in Cybersecurity 383","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 11: Applying LLMs in Cybersecurity 383\nReframing cybersecurity with LLMs ������������������������������������������������������������������������������� 383\nAdvantages of LLMs in the field of security • 384\nChallenges of LLMs in the field of security • 384\nResponding to challenges of LLM technologies • 385\nApplying LLMs across security workflows ���������������������������������������������������������������������� 385\nAnomaly detection • 386\nThreat identification and classification • 386\nAutomated threat hunting • 387\nPhishing attack identification • 388\nMalware detection • 388\nIntrusion detection system • 389\nSecurity policy recommendations • 390\nPredictive threat modeling • 390\nData leakage prevention • 391\nSafety education and training • 391\nIntelligence sharing and collaboration • 392\nCompliance monitoring • 392\nManaging the security risks of LLMs ������������������������������������������������������������������������������� 393\nInherent risks • 393\nAccess control vulnerabilities • 393\nTable of Contents xxi\nPrompt injection • 393\nContent hallucinations and poisoning of training data • 394\nApplication security risks • 394\nData leakage • 394\nAI ethics and morality • 394\nPrompt jailbreaks • 395\nSupply chain security • 395\nCounteracting risks • 395\nData lifecycle principles • 395\nData noise addition techniques • 395\nZero trust principles for access control • 396\nEvaluating zero-shot vulnerability repair with LLMs ����������������������������������������������������� 396\nResearch background • 396\nResearch approach • 397\nExperimental process • 398\nGeneration of vulnerable programs • 399\nTesting LLM Vulnerability Repair • 400\nTesting prompt context for vulnerability repair • 402\nPrompt design for vulnerability repair • 402\nAssessing the reliability of generated repair code • 404\nSummary ������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������ 405\nOther Books You May Enjoy 409\nPreface\nLarge Language Models (LLMs) have transformed natural language processing and accelerated\nthe rise of domain-specific foundation models. Yet inside software delivery organizations, the\npractical story is more complex. Many enterprises are still looking for convincing, repeatable\nways to make LLMs useful in production, not just in demonstrations or isolated experiments.\nOver the last decade, software delivery itself has evolved significantly. DevOps improved build,\ndeployment, and collaboration practices. DevSecOps, DataOps, NoOps, MLOps, and related\npractices extended this delivery ecosystem. Site Reliability Engineering renewed the focus on\nreliability, stability, operational rigor, and business continuity. Platform Engineering then emerged\nas a broader organizational approach, aiming to connect tools, workflows, and teams through a","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":21,"pageEnd":24,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::15","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 11: Applying LLMs in Cybersecurity 383","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"more unified, business-aware internal platform.\nHowever, as organizations scale, the software delivery chain has also become more complex.\nDelivery engineers, operations teams, testers, developers, security engineers, and project managers\nare often required to work across an expanding range of tools, frameworks, processes, and\nspecialized workflows. This creates a growing cognitive load. The problem is no longer only a lack\nof tools or features; it is the burden of learning, connecting, and operating too many fragmented\nsystems while still being expected to deliver reliable software and business value.\nLLMs do not automatically solve this problem. Adding another chatbot or standalone model\ninterface will not, by itself, reduce tool sprawl or simplify delivery work. What is needed is an\nintegrating platform approach: one that centralizes delivery data, curates software-delivery-\nspecific corpora and telemetry, grounds models through retrieval and guardrails, and embeds\nAI assistance into the delivery platform itself. In this view, AI support must become part of real\ndelivery work, including coding, testing, releasing, operating, securing, and coordinating projects,\nwith measurable outcomes, governance, and safe rollback.\nThis book is an attempt to close the gap between model capability and delivery reality. It moves\nfrom model mechanics to enterprise readiness, and then to practical software delivery scenarios\nthat can be evaluated and adapted.\nPreface xxiv\nThe goal is to help you understand not only how LLMs work, but also where they can create\nmeasurable value inside software delivery organizations, and how they can be introduced without\nincreasing complexity or risk.\nWho this book is for\nThis book is for engineers, technical leads, and practitioners working in or around DevOps, SRE,\nand Platform Engineering who want to understand where LLMs fit in modern software delivery\nwithout making existing workflows heavier or harder to manage.\nIt is also for anyone operating along the software delivery chain, including developers, testers,\nsecurity engineers, project managers, requirements and product roles, SRE and operations\nengineers, and delivery coordinators. If your work involves pipelines, repositories, reviews,\nincidents, tickets, requirements, releases, testing, security, or operational workflows, this book\nis intended to help you separate durable LLM-enabled practices from hype.\nThe book is also relevant for LLM practitioners who understand models but need to make them\nwork within change-management, delivery, reliability, and governance constraints. It can also\nsupport academic programs and training courses focused on AI-assisted software engineering,\nAI-assisted delivery, or modern platform organizations.\nWhat this book covers","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":24,"pageEnd":25,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::16","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 1, Understanding the Foundations of Large Language Models, traces the development of","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 1, Understanding the Foundations of Large Language Models, traces the development of\nlanguage models from early theoretical and statistical approaches to neural sequence models\nand LLMs. You will learn how N-gram models, HMMs, RNNs, LSTMs, and attention shaped the\npath toward modern large language models.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":25,"pageEnd":25,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::17","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 2, Understanding the Transformer Architecture Behind Large Language Models, explains why the","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2, Understanding the Transformer Architecture Behind Large Language Models, explains why the\nTransformer became the foundation of modern LLMs. You will learn how embeddings, positional\nencoding, self-attention, multi-head attention, feed-forward layers, residual connections, and\nlayer normalization work together inside the Transformer architecture.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":25,"pageEnd":25,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::18","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 3, Tracing the Path from Bigram Models to GPT and ChatGPT, connects the Transformer","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 3, Tracing the Path from Bigram Models to GPT and ChatGPT, connects the Transformer\narchitecture to GPT-style language generation. You will learn how a simple bigram model\nillustrates next-token prediction, how GPT models generate text, and how training and generation\ntechniques can be improved.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":25,"pageEnd":25,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::19","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 4, Applying Efficient Fine-Tuning Techniques to LLMs, introduces the main ways pre-trained","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 4, Applying Efficient Fine-Tuning Techniques to LLMs, introduces the main ways pre-trained\nLLMs can be adapted to specific tasks and domains. You will learn about full-parameter fine-\ntuning, parameter-efficient fine-tuning, PEFT, LoRA, QLoRA, and practical fine-tuning workflows.\nPreface xxv","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":25,"pageEnd":26,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::20","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 5, Building Enterprise AI Applications with RAG and Multi-Agent Systems, Building Enterprise","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 5, Building Enterprise AI Applications with RAG and Multi-Agent Systems, Building Enterprise\nAI Applications with RAG and Multi-Agent Systems, shows how LLMs can be grounded in\nenterprise knowledge and connected to business workflows. You will learn how RAG works, how\nit addresses enterprise constraints such as accuracy and domain knowledge, and how multi-agent\nsystems can coordinate more complex tasks.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":26,"pageEnd":26,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::21","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 6, Building a Modern Software Delivery Foundation, Building a Modern Software Delivery","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 6, Building a Modern Software Delivery Foundation, Building a Modern Software Delivery\nFoundation, explains the delivery environment in which enterprise LLM capabilities must operate.\nYou will learn how DevOps, Platform Engineering, and SRE support collaboration, automation,\nreliability, observability, and value delivery across modern software organizations.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":26,"pageEnd":26,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::22","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 7, Applying LLMs in Intelligent Operations and Maintenance, Applying LLMs in Intelligent","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 7, Applying LLMs in Intelligent Operations and Maintenance, Applying LLMs in Intelligent\nOperations and Maintenance, explores how LLMs can support operations and maintenance work.\nYou will learn how LLMs can improve log analysis, intelligent O&M knowledge bases, ticket\nhandling, capability evaluation, and multi-agent root cause analysis.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":26,"pageEnd":26,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::23","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 8, Applying LLMs in Software Testing, examines the role of LLMs in dynamic and static","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 8, Applying LLMs in Software Testing, examines the role of LLMs in dynamic and static\nsoftware testing. You will learn how LLMs can support test generation, static analysis, vulnerability\nrepair, deployment challenges, and the evaluation of AI-assisted testing workflows.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":26,"pageEnd":26,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::24","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 9, Applying Code LLMs in Software Development, focuses on code-focused LLMs and their","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 9, Applying Code LLMs in Software Development, focuses on code-focused LLMs and their\nuse in development workflows. You will learn about large code models, representative code LLMs,\ndownstream coding tasks, code generation and completion, and agent-based approaches to\nproject-level code generation.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":26,"pageEnd":26,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::25","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 10, Applying LLMs in Project Management, shows how LLMs can support project delivery","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 10, Applying LLMs in Project Management, shows how LLMs can support project delivery\nand coordination. You will learn how LLMs can assist with requirements analysis, task planning,\ncommunication, collaboration, risk management, decision support, intelligent workflows, and\nproject management case studies.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":26,"pageEnd":26,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::26","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 11, Applying LLMs in Cybersecurity, explores how LLMs can be applied to security workflows","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 11, Applying LLMs in Cybersecurity, explores how LLMs can be applied to security workflows\nwhile also introducing new risks. You will learn how LLMs can support threat detection, security\noperations, alert triage, vulnerability repair, and the management of security risks introduced\nby LLM adoption.\nTo get the most out of this book\nYou do not need a research lab to use this book. You should, however, have a working intuition\nfor how software delivery teams operate. Familiarity with delivery pipelines, code repositories,\ncode reviews, incidents, tickets, testing workflows, operations, and security practices will help\nyou connect the book’s concepts to real organizational settings.\nPreface xxvi\nThe book was originally developed from Chinese-language material and has been translated and\nadapted for a global audience, with examples and explanations adjusted where needed to make\nthe concepts more accessible across different software delivery contexts.\nThe book assumes that you want to understand both the model side and the delivery side of LLM\nadoption. You will benefit from a basic awareness of LLM concepts such as Transformers, token\nbudgets, fine-tuning, and Retrieval-Augmented Generation, but the early chapters introduce\nthe necessary foundation before moving into applied delivery scenarios. If these concepts are\nalready familiar, you may move more quickly through the foundation chapters and focus on the\npractice chapters.\nCode examples and implementation patterns are presented so they can be adapted to mainstream\nsoftware delivery stacks. When a concept depends on an infrastructure assumption, that\nassumption is called out. The emphasis is not on one fixed toolchain, but on helping you\nunderstand the principles, constraints, workflows, and evaluation points needed to apply LLMs\nresponsibly in software delivery contexts.\nDownload the example code files\nThe code bundle for the book is hosted on GitHub at https://github.com/PacktPublishing/\nLLMs-for-Modern-Software-Delivery-and-DevOps. We also have other code bundles from our\nrich catalog of books and videos available at https://github.com/PacktPublishing. Check\nthem out!\nDownload the color images\nWe also provide a PDF file that has color images of the screenshots/diagrams used in this book.\nYou can download it here: https://packt.link/gbp/9781807609191.\nConventions used\nThere are a number of text conventions used throughout this book.\nCodeInText: Indicates code words in text, database table names, folder names, filenames, file\nextensions, pathnames, dummy URLs, user input, and Twitter handles. For example: “For real\napplications, libraries such as tiktoken or sentencepiece can be used to implement more practical\ntokenization workflows.”\nA block of code is set as follows:\n# Set random seed\ndef set_seed(seed_value=42):","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":26,"pageEnd":27,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::27","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 11, Applying LLMs in Cybersecurity, explores how LLMs can be applied to security workflows","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Preface xxvii\ntorch.manual_seed(seed_value)\ntorch.cuda.manual_seed(seed_value)\ntorch.cuda.manual_seed_all(seed_value) # If using multiple GPUs\ntorch.backends.cudnn.deterministic = True\ntorch.backends.cudnn.benchmark = False\nnp.random.seed(seed_value)\nrandom.seed(seed_value\nAny command-line input or output is written as follows:\n[2024-07-10 22:57:04][DEBUG]: == Working Agent: Researcher\n[2024-07-10 22:57:04][INFO]: == Starting Task: Research the latest\ntechnical trends related to DevOps and write\nA report\nBold: Indicates a new term, an important word, or words that you see on the screen. For instance,\nwords in menus or dialog boxes appear in the text like this. For example: “ GPT stands for Generative\nPre-trained Transformer ”\nGet in touch\nFeedback from our readers is always welcome.\nGeneral feedback: If you have questions about any aspect of this book or have any general feedback,\nplease email us at customercare@packt.com and mention the book’s title in the subject of your\nmessage.\nErrata: Although we have taken every care to ensure the accuracy of our content, mistakes do\nhappen. If you have found a mistake in this book, we would be grateful if you reported this to us.\nPlease visit http://www.packt.com/submit-errata, click Submit Errata, and fill in the form.\nPiracy: If you come across any illegal copies of our works in any form on the internet, we would\nbe grateful if you would provide us with the location address or website name. Please contact us\nat copyright@packt.com with a link to the material.\nIf you are interested in becoming an author: If there is a topic that you have expertise in and you\nare interested in either writing or contributing to a book, please visit http://authors.packt.com/.\nWarnings or important notes appear like this.\nStay Sharp in Cloud and DevOps – Join 44,000+\nSubscribers of CloudPro\nCloudPro is a weekly newsletter for cloud professionals who want to stay current on the fast-\nevolving world of cloud computing, DevOps, and infrastructure engineering.\nEvery issue delivers focused, high-signal content on topics like:\n• AWS, GCP & multi-cloud architecture\n• Containers, Kubernetes & orchestration\n• Infrastructure as Code (IaC) with Terraform, Pulumi, etc.\n• Platform engineering & automation workflows\n• Observability, performance tuning, and reliability best practices\nWhether you’re a cloud engineer, SRE, DevOps practitioner, or platform lead, CloudPro helps you\nstay on top of what matters, without the noise.\nScan the QR code to join for free and get weekly insights straight to your inbox:\nhttps://packt.link/cloudpro\nShare your thoughts\nOnce you’ve read LLMs for Modern Software Delivery and DevOps, we’d love to hear your thoughts!\nPlease click here to go straight to the Amazon review page for this book and share\nyour feedback.\nYour review is important to us and the tech community and will help us make sure we’re delivering","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":28,"pageEnd":30,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::28","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 11, Applying LLMs in Cybersecurity, explores how LLMs can be applied to security workflows","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"excellent quality content.\nPart 1\nFoundations of Large\nLanguage Models\nIn this first part, you will build the technical foundation needed to understand how large language\nmodels work and how they are adapted for practical use. You will begin by tracing the evolution\nof language modeling from early statistical and sequence-based approaches to modern LLMs.\nYou will then examine the Transformer architecture, including embeddings, positional encoding,\nattention, self-attention, and multi-head attention, before moving into GPT-style generation\nand ChatGPT.\nThis foundation also includes fine-tuning, because understanding LLMs is not complete without\nunderstanding how pre-trained models are adapted to specific tasks and domains. You will learn\nhow techniques such as PEFT, LoRA, and QLoRA make adaptation more efficient and practical.\nThis part of the book includes the following chapters:\n• Chapter 1, Understanding the Foundations of Large Language Models\n• Chapter 2, Understanding the Transformer Architecture Behind Large Language Models\n• Chapter 3, Tracing the Path from Bigram Models to GPT and ChatGPT\n• Chapter 4, Applying Efficient Fine-Tuning Techniques to LLMs\n1\nUnderstanding the Foundations\nof Large Language Models\nLarge language models (LLMs) did not appear suddenly. They are the result of decades of progress\nin natural language processing, from early theoretical models of information and probability to\nstatistical language models, neural networks, and large-scale pre-trained systems such as BERT\nand GPT. Understanding this development path is important because it shows what problems\nearlier models tried to solve, where they were limited, and why modern LLMs represent such a\nmajor shift in how machines process language.\nThis chapter introduces the foundations of LLMs by tracing the evolution of language modeling\ntechniques. You will first examine the major stages in the development of natural language\nprocessing, then explore important pre-LLM models such as N-gram models, Hidden Markov\nModels, RNNs, and LSTMs. Finally, you will compare HMMs and N-gram models to understand\nhow different modeling assumptions shape the way language systems process sequences.\nIn this chapter, we will be covering the following main topics:\n• Tracing the development of LLMs\n• Exploring foundational NLP models before LLMs\n• Introducing neural networks for sequence modeling\n• Addressing long-sequence challenges with LSTMs\nUnderstanding the Foundations of Large Language Models 4\nTracing the development of LLMs\nIn 1775, James Watt improved the steam engine, successfully solving a core problem in the process\nof industrialization and ushering in a new chapter in the Industrial Revolution. In 1879, Thomas\nEdison invented the light bulb, bringing light to the darkness. In 1882, the world’s first commercial","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":30,"pageEnd":35,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::29","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 11, Applying LLMs in Cybersecurity, explores how LLMs can be applied to security workflows","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"power plant was built, laying the foundation for the modern electrical system. In 1887, Nikola\nTesla developed the alternating current system. These great inventions not only changed the way\npeople lived but also propelled societal progress.\nEntering the 20th century, technology embarked on an entirely new phase. In 1946, the advent of\nElectronic Numerical Integrator and Computer (ENIAC) marked the beginning of the electronic\ncomputer era, thus ushering humanity into the computer age. In 1975, the appearance of the Altair\n8800 brought computer technology into the personal domain for the first time, kicking off the era\nof the personal computer. A series of subsequent technological advancements greatly accelerated\nthe arrival of the Information Age. In 1989, the birth of the World Wide Web made information\nexchange unprecedentedly convenient; in 1998, the founding of Google further fueled the rapid\ndevelopment of the Internet era.\nAfter entering the 21st century, Apple released the first iPhone in 2007, swiftly heralding the era of\nmobile internet. This once again led to a dramatic transformation in the way people lived and how\nthey accessed information, until 2020, when OpenAI released GPT-3 — an event that marked the\nfull arrival of the era of artificial intelligence. The cycle has compressed from centuries to decades.\nThe arrival of the AI era is an important turning point in the development of human society. In\nthis new era, whether as individuals or organizations, practitioners slow to engage will find the\ngap harder to close. AI models are the core element of the new era, and technology practitioners\nneed to be thorough with their background knowledge of this technology in order to better adapt\nto and drive the development of the new age.\nTo understand how LLMs became possible, it is useful to look at the major stages of natural\nlanguage processing (NLP) that came before them. Each stage introduced a different way of\nmodeling language, moving from theoretical foundations to statistical methods, neural networks,\nand finally large-scale pre-trained language models.\nMapping the evolution of NLP models\nBefore LLMs emerged, NLP models experienced several stages of development. The following\nare some crucial stages.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":35,"pageEnd":35,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::30","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 1 5","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 1 5\nEstablishing theoretical foundations for language models\nTheoretical models, represented by Shannon’s information theory, study the information\nquantity and entropy of natural language. In 1948, Shannon applied Markov process models to\nnatural language modeling and proposed extending the concept of entropy from thermodynamics\nto the field of natural language modeling.\nThese early theoretical ideas provided a foundation for treating language as information that\ncould be measured and modeled. As computing resources became available, researchers began\napplying statistical methods to model word sequences and speech patterns more directly.\nApplying statistical models to natural language processing\nThe representative models of this stage are the N-gram model and the Hidden Markov Model\n(HMM). The N-gram model is a relatively simple statistical language model, which assumes that\nthe probability of a word occurring depends only on the previous N-1 words. It is used for text\nprediction. HMM is a statistical model for handling sequential data and is suitable for tasks such\nas speech recognition and machine translation.\nStatistical models made language processing more practical, but they still relied heavily on fixed\nassumptions and manually designed probabilities. As machine learning advanced, neural network\nmodels offered a way to learn richer representations from data.\nIntroducing neural networks for sequence modeling\nThe representative models of this stage are the Recurrent Neural Network (RNN) and the\nConvolutional Neural Network (CNN). RNNs can handle sequential data and are suitable for\ntasks such as machine translation and speech recognition. CNNs can process image data and are\nsuitable for text classification, sentiment analysis, and other tasks.\nNeural network models improved the ability to process sequences and learn patterns from\nlanguage, but larger datasets and greater computing power enabled a further shift. This led to\nthe development of pre-trained language models and eventually LLMs, which could learn broad\nlanguage representations from massive corpora.\nScaling toward LLMs\nRepresentative models of this stage include BERT and GPT-3, among others. BERT is a pre-trained\nlanguage model released by Google AI in 2018, and it has attracted widespread attention due to\nits outstanding language understanding capabilities. The advent of BERT marks the transition\nof the natural language processing field from traditional manual feature engineering to the deep\nlearning era.\nUnderstanding the Foundations of Large Language Models 6\nAccording to the implementation sequence of key algorithmic models, the development trajectory\nof natural language models is shown in Figure 1.1. The graph shows the history of NLP models\nand the required computing power from 1950 to 2020. The computational power data in Figure","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":36,"pageEnd":37,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::31","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 1 5","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"1.1 come from Wikipedia.\nFigure 1.1: The development trajectory of natural language models\nAs explained above, NLP is founded on theory. With limited computing power, statistical methods\nwere employed to model natural language. As computing power increased, neural network models\nwere gradually applied to the field of natural language processing. With the realization of even\ngreater computational power, LLMs emerged, marking the entrance of natural language processing\ninto the AI era.\nAlthough we have entered the era of LLMs, traditional natural language processing models still\npossess unique advantages, such as low computational resource consumption and fast response\nspeed. In practical applications, the appropriate model should be selected based on the specific\ntask requirements.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":37,"pageEnd":37,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::32","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 1 7","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 1 7\nWith this historical progression in mind, the next section looks more closely at the foundational\nmodels that shaped NLP before the rise of modern LLMs. Understanding models such as N-grams,\nHMMs, RNNs, and LSTMs will make it easier to see what problems LLMs were designed to\novercome and why earlier approaches still matter in some practical scenarios.\nExploring foundational NLP models before LLMs\nBefore modern LLMs, NLP relied on simpler statistical and sequence-based models to represent\nlanguage, predict words, and infer hidden patterns from observed data. This section introduces\ntwo important foundational approaches: N-gram models, which predict language based on\nobserved word frequencies, and Hidden Markov Models (HMM), which use hidden states and\ntransition probabilities to model sequences. Understanding these models helps clarify what\nearlier NLP systems could do well, where they were limited, and why later neural models became\nnecessary.\nUnderstanding N-gram models\nIn the field of natural language processing, the N-gram model is a relatively simple statistical\nlanguage model. It is based on the Markov assumption, which means the probability of a word\nappearing depends only on the previous N-1 words. The formula for the N-gram model is as follows:\n𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃 𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃 𝑃 𝑃 𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃 𝑃 𝑃 𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃 𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃 𝑃 𝑃 𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃 𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃 𝑃 𝑃 𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃𝑃\nIn this equation, w 1, w 2, …, w n is a word sequence of length n, C (w 1, w 2, …, w n) is the number of\noccurrences of this sequence in the corpus, and C (w 1, w 2, …, w n-1) is the number of occurrences of\nthe previous n-1 words of this sequence in the corpus. The number of parameters in the N-gram\nmodel increases exponentially with N, so in practice, 2-gram or 3-gram models are usually used.\nFrom the perspective of understanding the basic concepts, we can temporarily ignore the complex\nprinciples behind the formula and directly discuss how the N-gram model is used in practice.\nSuppose there is a chain composed of multiple words; the N-gram algorithm observes units\nmade up of every N consecutive words in that chain to predict which word may appear in the next\ngroup of words. This is where the name “N-gram” comes from, with “gram” referring to a word.\nFor example, when you say the phrase “I like to eat,” and there is previously a phrase like “I like to\neat apples,” the N-gram model may predict the next word to be “apples” because in previous data,\n“apples” often appears after “I like to eat.” This is why the N-gram model is used for text prediction.\nUnderstanding the Foundations of Large Language Models 8\nThe design of the N-gram model is quite straightforward: it is similar to a large table that records\nthe frequency of different word combinations. For a 2-gram (i.e., Bigram), the table records the","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":38,"pageEnd":39,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::33","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 1 7","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"probability of every pair of words; for a 3-gram (i.e., Trigram), it records the probability for every\nthree-word combination, and so on. When designing this model, you only need to determine the\nvalue of N, that is, how many words to consider as a group to predict the next word, then collect\ndata and fill in the probability table.\nAlthough the N-gram model is somewhat outdated, it still performs well in certain specific\nscenarios. For example, in some vertical domains, the N-gram model can achieve good results\nthrough training on large amounts of data. In resource-limited situations, the N-gram model can\nachieve satisfactory results with less training data. In scenarios that require a fast response time,\nthe N-gram model can deliver good results with relatively little computation.\nApplying N-gram models in practical scenarios\nBecause N-gram models are simple, fast, and based on frequency statistics, they remain useful in\ntasks where lightweight prediction is more important than deep contextual understanding. The\nfollowing examples show where this type of model can still be applied effectively.\nSpelling check and error correction\nThe N-gram model can be used to predict the probability of word sequences, thereby supporting\ntasks such as speech recognition and machine translation. By comparing the probabilities of\ndifferent word sequences, it is possible to infer which sequence is more likely to be the user’s\nintended input. For example, if a user types “spel checker”, the N-gram model can determine\nthat the sequence “spell checker” is more common than “spel checker”, thus suggesting the\ncorrect spelling.\nSo, in spelling correction, the model compares the likelihood of different word sequences and\nselects the one that appears more probable based on previous data. A similar principle can also be\napplied to simple text generation, where the model predicts likely next words from recent input.\nText generation\nAlthough deep learning models have made significant progress in text generation, the N-gram\nmodel still has practical applications in certain simple scenarios or resource-constrained\nsituations. For instance, in predictive text functions for keyboard applications or auto-reply for\nsimple chatbots, the N-gram model can quickly generate text suggestions based on previous inputs.\nBeyond these examples, the main strengths and limitations of N-gram models can be summarized\nthrough their key characteristics.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":39,"pageEnd":39,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::34","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 1 9","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 1 9\nCharacteristics of N-gram models\nThe above examples show how N-gram models use frequency patterns for lightweight prediction.\nTheir main strengths and limitations can be summarized through the following characteristics:\n• Prediction based on frequency statistics: The N-gram model predicts the next word\naccording to the occurrence probability of lexical sequences. The model assumes that the\nappearance of the current word depends only on the previous N-1 words.\n• The N-gram model is stateless: In the N-gram model, there is no explicit definition of\n“state”; the model directly operates on the observed sequences (such as words in text).\nEach part of the sequence is considered independently.\n• Simplicity and computational efficiency: The design and implementation of the N-gram\nmodel are relatively simple, and computational efficiency is high, but as N increases, the\nmodel’s size and complexity grow dramatically.\nN-gram models work directly with observed word sequences and use frequency statistics to\npredict what may come next. Hidden Markov Models take a different approach: they assume\nthat an observed sequence is generated by an underlying sequence of hidden states. This makes\nHMMs useful for tasks where the system must infer something not directly visible from the data\nthat can be observed.\nUnderstanding Hidden Markov Models\nApart from the N-gram model based on statistical thinking, the Hidden Markov Model (HMM)\nis also an important model in natural language processing. The basic idea of HMM is to assume\nthat the observed sequence is generated by an unobservable state sequence, and the transitions\nof the state sequence follow a Markov chain. The formula of HMM is as follows:\n𝑃𝑃(𝑂𝑂 𝑂 𝑂𝑂) = ∑\n𝑄𝑄\n𝑃𝑃(𝑂𝑂|𝑄𝑄)𝑃𝑃 (𝑄𝑄|𝑂𝑂) \nIn this equation, P(Q|I) is the probability of the state sequence Q under condition I, and P(O|Q)\nis the probability of the observed sequence O given the state sequence Q. The purpose of the\nsummation is to compute the probability of the observed sequence O under all possible state\nsequences Q. The parameters of HMM include the state transition probability matrix, observation\nprobability matrix, and initial state probability vector. The training process of HMM usually uses\nthe Baum-Welch algorithm, and the decoding process generally uses the Viterbi algorithm.\nUnderstanding the Foundations of Large Language Models 10\nThe above formulas and algorithms may seem rather abstract, but you can better understand the\nusage of HMM from practical application perspectives.\nApplying HMMs in practical scenarios\nThe idea of hidden states becomes easier to understand through practical examples. In each case,\nthe model observes available evidence and uses it to infer an underlying condition or intention\nthat cannot be seen directly.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":40,"pageEnd":41,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::35","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 1 9","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Weather forecasting\nImagine you are an ancient meteorologist who cannot directly know whether tomorrow’s weather\nwill be sunny, cloudy, or rainy. However, you can infer tomorrow’s weather by observing natural\nphenomena, such as bird behavior, wind direction, and the shape of clouds. In this scenario,\ntomorrow’s weather (sunny, cloudy, rainy) belongs to the hidden states, as you cannot directly\naccess it; whereas the behaviors of birds, wind direction, and cloud formations are observable\nstates, as these can be directly observed. HMM can help you predict tomorrow’s weather (hidden\nstate) based on the observed natural phenomena (observable states). By learning from historical\ndata, HMM understands the transition probabilities between different weather states (for example,\nthe probability that a sunny day turns into a cloudy day) as well as the probability of observing\ncertain natural phenomena under specific weather conditions (such as the probability of seeing\nspecific bird behaviors on a Sunny day). Therefore, even if you cannot directly observe tomorrow’s\nweather, you can still make the most likely prediction based on today’s observations.\nWeather forecasting illustrates the general idea of hidden-state inference: the model uses visible\nclues to estimate an unseen condition. A similar idea can be applied to text input, where the system\nobserves what the user has typed and infers the most likely intended continuation.\nIntelligent recommendations\nWhen using the keyboard app on a smartphone and you start typing “I want to eat,” the keyboard\nautomatically suggests possible words for you, such as “apple,” “pizza,” or “ice cream.” In this\ncontext, the keyboard app uses HMM to predict which word you are most likely to type next. In this\nexample, the sequence of words you actually intend to enter (like “I want to eat pizza”) belongs\nto the hidden states, because the keyboard app cannot directly access your thoughts, while the\nwords you have actually typed (“I want to eat”) are the observable states, which the app can\ndirectly obtain. By analyzing large amounts of text data, HMM learns the transition probabilities\nbetween words (for example, the probability that “I want to eat” is followed by “pizza”) as well\nas the probability that the next word appears given a sequence. Therefore, even though the app\ndoes not know your exact intention, it can use your input and the learned language model to\nrecommend the most suitable word for you.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":41,"pageEnd":41,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::36","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 1 11","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 1 11\nN-gram models and HMMs are both effective tools for processing sequence data, widely used in\nNLP and other fields. Although there are certain similarities between them, they have significant\ndifferences in theoretical foundations and applications. HMMs focus on state transition\nprobabilities and the probability of observations given a state. By analyzing observed data, HMMs\ninfer the sequence of hidden states that cannot be directly observed and are commonly used in\napplication scenarios such as weather prediction and text input.\nCharacteristics of HMMs\nFrom these examples, we can identify the features that distinguish HMMs from simpler sequence\nmodels:\n• State-based and transitions: HMM is a state-based probabilistic model, where the\nsequence of states is hidden and cannot be directly observed. The model infers these\nhidden states from the observed sequence (such as words or phonemes).\n• HMMs have states: HMMs define hidden states and the transition probabilities between\nthese states. Each state is associated with a set of possible observations, which are defined\nby an observation probability distribution.\n• Complexity and powerful modeling capability: HMMs can model the complex processes\nunderlying observed sequences. Although their training and inference are more complex\nthan N-gram models, they are able to capture deeper sequential dependencies.\nN-gram models and HMMs both process sequential data, but they differ in how they represent\nthat data. N-gram models work directly with observed word sequences, while HMMs separate\nobserved signals from hidden states. The next section compares these two approaches in terms\nof observations, states, dependencies, and typical application areas.\nComparing HMMs and N-gram models\nThe main theoretical differences between HMM and N-gram models are as follows:\n• Observations and states: N-gram models act directly on the observed sequence, whereas\nHMMs distinguish between the observed sequence and the hidden state sequence.\n• Dependencies: The dependencies in N-gram models are relatively simple and limited\nto a fixed length (N-1), while the state transitions in HMMs can capture longer-term\ndependencies, and the model structure allows for more flexible sequential dependency\nmodeling.\nUnderstanding the Foundations of Large Language Models 12\n• Range of application: Due to these differences, HMMs are generally used for more complex\ntasks, such as speech recognition or sequence alignment in bioinformatics, where the\nmodel needs to infer underlying structures behind observed data. N-gram models, on\nthe other hand, are more suitable for tasks that directly predict based on sequential data,\nsuch as text generation or simpler language models.\nN-gram models and HMMs show how earlier NLP systems handled sequences through frequency","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":42,"pageEnd":43,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::37","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 1 11","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"statistics, hidden states, and transition probabilities. However, these approaches relied on fixed\nassumptions and could not learn flexible contextual representations directly from large amounts\nof data. Neural network models introduced a new direction by learning sequence patterns from\ndata, which is the focus of the next section.\nIntroducing neural networks for sequence modeling\nEarly developments of statistical models only considered frequency statistics and did not fully\ntake into account the temporal relationships in sequence data. HMMs introduced the concept\nof state transition probabilities, thereby gaining a preliminary ability to predict based on state\nchanges. However, the state transition probabilities in HMMs are fixed and cannot be dynamically\nadjusted based on the temporal dependencies in sequence data. For time series processing tasks,\nsuch as language modeling, speech recognition, or any form of time series analysis, the stateless\nnature of HMMs limits their effectiveness, as processing sequence data often relies on contextual\ninformation. The design inspiration for Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) comes from the way\nthe human brain processes sequential information, especially its ability to maintain information\nstates through time. By introducing recurrence, RNNs are able to retain information in their\ninternal states, enabling them to dynamically process varying sequence lengths and capture\ntemporal dependencies in time series data. This structure allows RNNs to pass information from\nthe past to the current task, resulting in more effective learning and prediction. The basic structure\nof an RNN is shown in Figure 1.2.\nFigure 1.2: Basic structure of an RNN","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":43,"pageEnd":43,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::38","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 1 13","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 1 13\nFigure 1.2 shows a simple RNN structure, illustrating three consecutive time steps as an RNN\nprocesses sequence data.\n• x t denotes the input at time step t. This is the current element of the sequence received\nby the network.\n• h represents the hidden layer of the network, which is the core of the RNN. The hidden\nlayer can transmit information between different time steps, thereby retaining information\nfrom previous inputs.\n• h t-1, h t, h t+1 respectively represent the hidden states at time steps t-1, t, and t+1. Each hidden\nstate h t is calculated based on the previous step’s hidden state h t-1 and the current input x t.\n• o denotes the output layer, which generates output at each time step.\n• o t-1, o t, o t+1 denote the outputs at time steps t-1, t, and t+1, respectively. The output is usually\ncomputed based on the current time step’s hidden state h t.\nThe function of an RNN is to process sequence data, making it particularly suitable for tasks such\nas time series analysis, speech recognition, and language modeling. By maintaining hidden states,\nRNNs can capture the temporal dependencies in sequences, thereby generating or predicting the\nnext element in the sequence. This structure enables RNNs to take previous contextual information\ninto account for more accurate predictions in sequential tasks.\nAlthough RNNs improved on earlier statistical models by maintaining information across time\nsteps, their recurrent structure also introduced training difficulties. These limitations become\nespecially clear when RNNs process long sequences, where gradients must pass through many\nrepeated time steps during training.\nIdentifying the limitations of RNNs\nRNNs have two major architectural limitations:\n• Vanishing gradient: RNNs are prone to the vanishing gradient problem during training.\nThis occurs because the gradients of RNNs are computed through BackPropagation\nThrough Time (BPTT), and during backpropagation, the gradient is repeatedly multiplied\nby a value less than 1, causing the gradient to gradually disappear.\n• Exploding Gradient: RNNs are also susceptible to the exploding gradient problem during\ntraining. This happens because the gradients are calculated through backpropagation\nthrough time, and in this process, the gradients are continually multiplied by a value\ngreater than 1, causing the gradients to rapidly explode.\nUnderstanding the Foundations of Large Language Models 14\nThese problems are mainly caused by the recurrent connections in the RNN structure and the\nuse of saturated activation functions such as tanh or sigmoid. In long sequences, as gradients are\nbackpropagated to earlier layers, the chain rule is applied multiple times, causing the gradients\nto be multiplied by a series of weight matrices and accumulated over multiple time steps. If the","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":44,"pageEnd":45,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::39","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 1 13","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"values in these weight matrices are less than 1, the gradients will gradually decay, leading to\nvanishing gradients; if the values are greater than 1, the gradients will grow rapidly, resulting in\nexploding gradients.\nThese training challenges made it difficult for standard RNNs to retain useful information over long\nsequences. LSTMs were introduced to address this problem by adding a memory cell and gating\nmechanisms that control how information is stored, updated, and passed forward through time.\nAddressing long-sequence challenges with LSTMs\nLSTM (Long Short-Term Memory) networks are a special type of RNN, specifically designed to\nsolve the problems of gradient vanishing and gradient explosion that occur when RNNs process\nlong sequence data. LSTM addresses these issues by introducing key structures. Figure 1.3 shows\na simple internal structure of an LSTM.\nFigure 1.3: Internal structure of LSTM\nTo understand how LSTMs address the limitations of standard RNNs, it is useful to examine the\ninternal components that control memory over time. The memory cell and its gates determine\nwhat information is retained, updated, forgotten, and passed forward through the sequence.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":45,"pageEnd":45,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::40","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 1 15","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 1 15\nExplaining the LSTM memory cell and gates\nThe core of LSTM is a structure called the memory cell, which can retain information over long\nsequence data. The memory cell consists of an internal state called the cell state and an external\nstate called the hidden state. The cell state can transmit information between time steps, thus\nmaintaining memory of previous inputs. The hidden state is calculated based on the current input\nand the hidden state from the previous time step. The memory cell of the LSTM also includes\nthree gates: forget gate, input gate, and output gate. These gates control the updating of the\ncell state and the output of the hidden state, allowing LSTMs to capture temporal dependencies\nin long sequence data:\n• Forget gate: Applies a sigmoid function to a linear transformation of the cell state from the\nprevious time step and the current time step’s input to compute the forget probability. This\nis then multiplied element-wise to decide which information to forget from the cell state.\n• Input gate: Uses sigmoid and tanh functions to control which information to update and\nhow to update it, combining the previous time step’s cell state and the current time step’s\ninput to form a new cell state.\n• Output gate: Uses a sigmoid function to perform a linear transformation of the current\ntime step’s cell state to determine the output probability, which is then multiplied element-\nwise to decide which information to output.\nThe roles of the forget gate, input gate, and output gate are not just to “decide” what operations to\nperform on certain information, but rather to control the flow of information via learned weights.\nIn addition to introducing the gating mechanism, LSTM also implements other improvements\nsuch as peephole connections and coupled forget gates:\n• Peephole connections: Through peephole connections, the forget gate can directly access\nthe memory cell state C t at the current time step to decide how much information to forget,\nthereby controlling the information flow more precisely.\n• Coupled forget gates allow for more effective handling of noise in the input sequence. A\ntypical application scenario is the “machine translation task.” LSTM can remember the\nsemantics and structure of sentences in the source language and retain this information\nduring translation, thus generating more accurate translation results.\nThese mechanisms improved the ability of recurrent models to preserve information over longer\nsequences, but they did not remove all practical limitations. Researchers therefore proposed\nrelated architectures and extensions that made recurrent sequence models more efficient, more\ncontext-aware, and better suited to complex language tasks.\nUnderstanding the Foundations of Large Language Models 16\nConnecting LSTM variants to attention","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":46,"pageEnd":47,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::41","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 1 15","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"LSTM also has some issues in practical applications, for which researchers have proposed several\nimproved models. Figure 1.4 shows three branch models of LSTM:\nFigure 1.4: Three branch models of LSTM\nThe three important branches of LSTM are described as follows:\n• Gated Recurrent Unit: A simplified version of LSTM with fewer parameters and faster\ntraining speed, suitable for scenarios that require higher speed and efficiency, such as\nspeech recognition.\n• Attention Mechanism: Enables the model to focus on the parts of the input sequence most\nrelevant to the current output, suitable for tasks like text summarization and machine\ntranslation.\n• Bidirectional Long Short-Term Memory Network: Able to process sequence data by\nsimultaneously utilizing both past and future information, suitable for scenarios that\nrequire modeling global dependencies in the input sequence, such as sentiment analysis\nand text classification.\nAmong these branches, the attention mechanism became especially important. It allowed\nsequence models to focus on the parts of the input that were most relevant to the current output,\nimproving performance on tasks such as summarization and machine translation. This idea was\nlater developed further in the Transformer architecture, where attention became the central\nmechanism for processing language.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":47,"pageEnd":47,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::42","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 1 17","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 1 17\nSummary\nYou now have a foundational understanding of how LLMs evolved and how earlier NLP techniques\nled to modern AI systems.\nLLMs are the result of a long technological progression—from early theoretical models and\nstatistical approaches like N-gram and Hidden Markov Models, to neural network architectures\nsuch as RNNs and LSTMs, and eventually to modern deep-learning-based language models like\nBERT and GPT.\nYou have learned the core principles, characteristics, and applications of key language modeling\ntechniques, as well as the theoretical differences between statistical and neural approaches. By\nunderstanding these foundations, you are better prepared to grasp how modern LLMs work and\nhow they fit into the broader evolution of artificial intelligence and natural language processing.\nWith this foundational understanding of how natural language processing evolved, you are now\nready to explore the architecture that powers today’s most advanced AI systems. In the next\nchapter, we will examine the Transformer model, the cornerstone of modern LLMs, and learn\nhow mechanisms such as attention, word embeddings, and positional encoding enable machines\nto process and understand language effectively.\n2\nUnderstanding the Transformer\nArchitecture Behind Large\nLanguage Models\nIn the field of modern natural language processing, with the rapid development of deep learning\ntechnology, traditional sequence models such as RNN and LSTM have gradually exposed efficiency\nand performance bottlenecks, especially when dealing with long-distance dependencies. The serial\ncomputation characteristic of these models leads to inefficiency in both training and inference\nprocesses. To address this issue, the Google AI team proposed the Transformer model in 2017.\nThis groundbreaking innovation has completely changed the way sequential data is processed.\nBy introducing the self-attention mechanism, the Transformer model not only overcomes the\nlimitations of traditional models but also lays the foundation for the future of natural language\nprocessing. Today, the latest large language models are all built on this architecture. An in-depth\nstudy of the principles and applications of the Transformer model is not only key to understanding\ncontemporary natural language processing technology but also an important step for the future\ndevelopment of artificial intelligence.\nWe will be covering the following main topics:\n• Tracing the origins of the transformer model\n• Understanding the core principles of the transformer architecture\n• Representing text with word embeddings\n• Encoding word order with positional information\n• Explaining the attention mechanism\nUnderstanding the Transformer Architecture Behind Large Language Models 20\n• Implementing transformer self-attention\n• Exploring the design of multi-head attention","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":48,"pageEnd":51,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::43","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 1 17","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"• Assembling the transformer architecture\nTracing the origins of the transformer model\nModels represented by RNNs and LSTMs perform excellently in handling sequential data. However,\ndue to their serial computing characteristics, they are inefficient during training and inference.\nThe fundamental reason lies in the processing logic of RNN and LSTM models, where the output\nat each time step depends not only on the current input but also on the output of the previous time\nstep. This means that the output for all previous time steps must be computed before calculating\nthe output at the current time step.\nMoreover, RNN models struggle with long-distance dependencies, as issues such as vanishing\nor exploding gradients make it difficult for the model to learn such dependencies. LSTM models\nalleviate the problems of vanishing or exploding gradients by introducing memory gate\nmechanisms, enabling them to handle long-distance dependencies more effectively.\nTo address this issue, the Google AI team proposed the Transformer model in 2017, a deep learning\nmodel designed for processing sequential data. The introduction of the Transformer marked a\nshift in the field of natural language processing from traditional models such as RNN, LSTM, and\nGRU to a new era of deep learning models capable of parallel training. Currently, the most popular\nlarge language models are all built based on the Transformer model. Therefore, understanding\nthe basic principles of the Transformer model is crucial for mastering the working mechanisms\nand application scenarios of large language models. To understand why the Transformer became\nthe foundation of modern large language models, it is useful to look next at the core principles of\nits architecture: how it represents input sequences, how attention captures relationships between\ntokens, and how the encoder and decoder work together to process language.\nUnderstanding the core principles of the transformer\narchitecture\nThe paper Attention is All You Need is the pioneering work of the Transformer model, proposing\na brand-new sequence modeling method—the Self-Attention Mechanism. Self-attention is a\ntechnique used to capture dependencies within input sequences, allowing the establishment of\nlong-range dependencies between different positions. Its core lies in calculating a weight for each\nposition in the input sequence, reflecting the dependency of that position with other positions.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":51,"pageEnd":51,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::44","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 2 21","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 21\nThis mechanism enables the model to capture long-range dependencies between different\npositions, thereby better handling dependencies in sequential data. The architecture of the model\nis shown in Figure 2.1.\nFigure 2.1: Architecture of the Transformer model\nUnderstanding the Transformer Architecture Behind Large Language Models 22\nThe Transformer model consists of an encoder and a decoder. The encoder is made up of\nmultiple stacked identical layers, each including a Multi-Head Attention Mechanism and a\nFeed-Forward Neural Network. The decoder is also composed of multiple stacked identical\nlayers, each comprising a multi-head attention mechanism, a feed-forward neural network, and\nan encoder-decoder attention mechanism. The encoder and decoder are connected via residual\nconnections and layer normalization. This structural design of the model can effectively capture\ndependencies within the input sequence, thus enhancing the expressive power of the model.\nTo understand how this architecture works in practice, it is useful to break the Transformer into\nthe main components that process input tokens, preserve sequence order, calculate contextual\nrelationships, and stabilize training. The success of the Transformer model relies on the following\nseven key technological innovations.\nWord embedding\nWord embedding is one of the core technologies of the Transformer model, capable of transforming\neach word in the input sequence into a vector to represent the semantic information of that word.\nThe core idea of word embedding is that the model generates a vector for each word in the input\nsequence, and these vectors are combined to represent the semantic information of the input\nsequence. The design of word embedding allows the model to better capture the semantic features\nin the input sequence, thereby improving the model’s expressive power.\nPositional encoding\nSince the Transformer model does not contain any recurrent relationships associated with\nposition, it requires a method to represent the positional information within the input sequence.\nPositional Encoding is a technique used to represent the position information in the input\nsequence, capable of generating a position vector for each position in the sequence to express its\npositional information. Positional encoding is an indispensable component of the Transformer\nmodel, compensating for its lack of a recursive structure.\nSelf-attention mechanism\nThe self-attention mechanism is the core of the Transformer model. Its core idea is that for\neach position in the input sequence, the model calculates a weight to represent the dependency\nrelationship between this position and other positions. This mechanism enables the model to\ncapture long-range dependencies between different positions, thus better understanding the","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":52,"pageEnd":53,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::45","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 2 21","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"dependency structure in sequential data.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":53,"pageEnd":53,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::46","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 2 23","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 23\nMulti-head attention mechanism\nThe multi-head attention mechanism is an extension of the self-attention mechanism, capable\nof capturing dependencies in the input sequence more effectively. Its core idea is that the model\ncomputes multiple different self-attention scores and merges these scores to represent the\ndependencies between different positions in the input sequence. The robustness and scalability\nof the multi-head attention mechanism enhance the stability of the model and make it extensible\nto other types of attention mechanisms.\nFeedforward neural network\nThe feedforward neural network is another core component of the Transformer model, capable\nof performing nonlinear transformations on each position of the input sequence. The core idea of\nthe feedforward neural network is to perform nonlinear transformations on each position of the\ninput sequence and integrate these transformation results to represent the feature information\nof the input sequence. The flexibility of the feedforward neural network allows it to be combined\nwith other types of network layers to achieve more complex functions.\nResidual connection\nResidual connection is one of the core designs in the Transformer model and can improve the\ntraining efficiency of the model. Its core idea is that the model adds the input and output of each\nposition in the input sequence, and uses the result as the input to the next layer. This design\nenables the model to better capture the feature information in the input sequence, thereby\nenhancing training efficiency.\nLayer normalization\nLayer normalization is one of the core techniques in the Transformer model and can improve the\ntraining efficiency of the model. Its core idea is that the model normalizes each position in the\ninput sequence and uses the normalized result as the input to the next layer. This design is widely\nused in various sub-layers of both the encoder and decoder to enhance the model’s capability of\nhandling sequences of different lengths and its generalization performance.\nTo understand how this architecture works in practice, it is useful to break the Transformer into\nthe main components that process input tokens, preserve sequence order, calculate contextual\nrelationships, and stabilize training.\nUnderstanding the Transformer Architecture Behind Large Language Models 24\nRepresenting text with word embeddings\nNeural networks cannot process raw text directly, so text must first be converted into numerical\nrepresentations. Word embeddings provide a way to represent words as dense vectors that can\ncapture semantic relationships between them. This section first explains why simple encoding\nmethods such as one-hot encoding are limited, then shows how word embeddings can be learned\nthrough a small neural network example and visualized in a two-dimensional space.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":54,"pageEnd":55,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::47","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 2 23","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Before examining how word embeddings learn semantic relationships, it is useful to begin with\nthe simplest question: how can words be represented as numbers at all?\nConverting text into numerical representations\nOne simple method is to use One-Hot encoding. One-Hot encoding is a technique that converts\ntext data into numerical data, enabling each word in the input sequence to be transformed into a\nvector to represent its semantics. The core idea of One-Hot encoding is that the model generates\na vector for each word in the input sequence and then combines these vectors to represent the\nsemantics of the input sequence.\nWe can explain the principle of One-Hot encoding with an example. Suppose we have a vocabulary\nwhere each word has a number, and each word can be represented by a vector in which each\ndimension corresponds to a word, as shown in Figure 2.2.\nFigure 2.2: Example of the concept of converting text to an encoded representation\nThis method has some limitations in that it cannot effectively capture the semantic relationships\nbetween words, as it only processes words independently and does not consider similarities\nbetween words.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":55,"pageEnd":55,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::48","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 2 25","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 25\nTo address this problem, we can use word embedding techniques. Words with similar meanings\nare closer in the word embedding space, while words with different meanings are farther apart.\nWord embedding technology maps each word into a continuous vector space so that the semantic\nrelationships between words can be captured more accurately. For example, words with similar\nmeanings are closer together in the word embedding space, while words with dissimilar meanings\nare farther apart. Suppose we have a simple text corpus containing only the following content:\nI like raising dogs.\nHe likes raising cats.\nHe likes planting trees.\nAfter preprocessing the text, the vocabulary obtained is: “I” “like” “raising” “dogs” “he” “cats”\n“planting” “trees.” We can use word embedding techniques to convert these words into vectors\nand visualize these vectors in a two-dimensional space, as shown in Figure 2.3.\nFigure 2.3: Schematic diagram of word embedding mapped to 2D space\nUnderstanding the Transformer Architecture Behind Large Language Models 26\nAlthough one-hot encoding can represent words numerically, it treats each word as independent\nand cannot capture similarity between words. Word embeddings address this limitation by\nlearning dense vector representations in which related words can appear closer together in vector\nspace.\nHow do word embeddings achieve this effect\nWe can regard word embedding as a learning task, constructing a probabilistic prediction model\nfor words using neural network models. To see how this works in practice, the following example\nbuilds a small word embedding model from a simple English corpus. The first step is to prepare\nthe environment, create the sample dataset, build the vocabulary, and convert word pairs into\ntraining examples.\nEnvironment preparation and data initialization\nThe following code demonstrates the basic data processing flow for word embeddings:\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport torch.optim as optim\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport random\nimport numpy as np\n# Set random seed\ndef set_seed(seed_value=42):\ntorch.manual_seed(seed_value)\ntorch.cuda.manual_seed(seed_value)\ntorch.cuda.manual_seed_all(seed_value) # If using multiple GPUs\ntorch.backends.cudnn.deterministic = True\ntorch.backends.cudnn.benchmark = False\nnp.random.seed(seed_value)\nrandom.seed(seed_value)\nset_seed(42) # Call the function and set the seed\n# Create a dataset from sentences\nraw_text_list = [\n'I like cats',\n'he likes dogs',","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":56,"pageEnd":57,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::49","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 2 27","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 27\n'he plants trees'\n]\n# Split sentences into word groups\nword_list = [sentence.split() for sentence in raw_text_list]\n# Flatten the word list into one large list\nraw_text = [word for sentence in word_list for word in sentence]\nvocab = set(raw_text)\nvocab_size = len(vocab)\nword_to_ix = {word: i for i, word in enumerate(vocab)}\nix_to_word = {i: word for i, word in enumerate(vocab)}\n# Create data pairs, each containing the current word and the next word\ndata = []\nfor sentence in word_list:\nfor i in range(len(sentence) - 1):\ncontext = sentence[i]\ntarget = sentence[i + 1]\ndata.append((context, target))\nThe above code covers the following aspects:\n• Data Processing: The raw text is split into words and converted into a series of (current\nword, next word) pairs. These pairs serve as the model’s input and output (target), and\nare used to analyze the probability distribution of each word and the next word during\nmodel training.\n• Vocabulary: All unique words form a vocabulary; each word is assigned a unique index.\n• Random Seed: Set the random seed to ensure reproducibility of the results. This is\nimportant because in deep learning, setting the random seed guarantees that the model’s\ntraining results are reproducible. However, since our dataset is too simple and may be\naffected by different programming system environments, you may get different results\nduring actual testing.\nAfter the dataset and vocabulary have been prepared, the next step is to define the neural network\nthat will learn the word embeddings. The model begins with an embedding layer, then uses linear\nlayers and an activation function to predict the next word from the current word.\nUnderstanding the Transformer Architecture Behind Large Language Models 28\nModel definition\n• Embedding Layer: The first layer of the model converts word indices into dense vectors\nof fixed size. These vectors are learnable and will be updated during training to better\ncapture the semantic relationships between words.\n• Linear Layers and Activation Function: The embedding vectors pass through two linear\nlayers and a ReLU activation function in sequence, forming the model’s hidden layers to\nenhance the model’s nonlinear expressive capability.\n• Output Layer: The final linear layer maps the output of the hidden layers to a vector\nwith the same size as the vocabulary. This vector is transformed into a probability\ndistribution using the log_softmax function, representing the probability of each word\nin the vocabulary being the next word, given the current word.\nclass Model(nn.Module):\ndef __init__(self, vocab_size, embed_size):\nsuper(Model, self).__init__()\nself.embeddings = nn.Embedding(vocab_size, embed_size)\nself.linear1 = nn.Linear(embed_size, 128)\nself.linear2 = nn.Linear(128, 64)\nself.linear3 = nn.Linear(64, vocab_size)","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":58,"pageEnd":59,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::50","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 2 27","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"self.activation = nn.ReLU()\ndef forward(self, inputs):\nembeds = self.embeddings(inputs)\nout = self.activation(self.linear1(embeds))\nout = self.activation(self.linear2(out))\nout = self.linear3(out)\nlog_probs = nn.functional.log_softmax(out, dim=1)\nreturn log_probs\n# Parameters","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":59,"pageEnd":59,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::51","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"EMBEDDING_DIM = 2","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"EMBEDDING_DIM = 2\nOnce the model structure has been defined, it can be trained on the word-pair data. During training,\nthe model compares its predicted next-word distribution with the actual next word, calculates\nthe loss, and updates the embedding vectors through backpropagation.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":59,"pageEnd":59,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::52","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 2 29","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 29\nTraining process\nFor each (current word, next word) pair, the current word’s index is used as input, and the model’s\nforward pass generates the probability distribution of the next word. Using the negative log\nlikelihood loss function (NLLLoss), the model’s predictions are compared with the actual next\nword to calculate the loss value. Through backpropagation and gradient descent (using the SGD\noptimizer), model parameters (including embedding vectors) are adjusted to reduce the loss.\n# Create model\nmodel = Model(vocab_size, EMBEDDING_DIM)\nloss_function = nn.NLLLoss()\noptimizer = optim.SGD(model.parameters(), lr=0.05)\n# Train model\nfor epoch in range(3000):\ntotal_loss = 0\nfor context, target in data:\ncontext_idx = torch.tensor([word_to_ix[context]], dtype=torch.\nlong)\nmodel.zero_grad()\nlog_probs = model(context_idx)\nloss = loss_function(log_probs, torch.tensor([word_to_ix[target]],\ndtype=torch.long))\nloss.backward()\noptimizer.step()\ntotal_loss += loss.item()\nif epoch % 50 == 0: # Print the loss value once every 50 epochs\nprint(f'Epoch {epoch}, Loss: {total_loss}')\nAfter training, the learned embedding vectors can be inspected visually. Plotting the vectors in\ntwo-dimensional space helps show whether the model has learned useful relationships between\nwords in the small training corpus.\nVisualization of word embeddings\nAfter training is completed, extract the embedding vector for each word and visualize them in\na two-dimensional space. The positions of these embedding vectors in space should reflect the\nsemantic relationships between words, such as similar words being mapped to locations close\nto each other.\nUnderstanding the Transformer Architecture Behind Large Language Models 30\n# Get word embeddings and plot\nembeddings = model.embeddings.weight.data.numpy()\nplt.figure(figsize=(8, 8))\nfor i, word in ix_to_word.items():\nplt.scatter(embeddings[i][0], embeddings[i][1])\nplt.annotate(\nword,\nxy=(embeddings[i][0], embeddings[i][1]),\nxytext=(5, 2),\ntextcoords='offset points',\nha='right',\nva='bottom'\n)\nplt.show()\nThe above code is primarily used to illustrate the main workflow of designing and training a\nword embedding model. It demonstrates the fundamental principles and training process of\nword embedding models from an experimental perspective. In practical applications, the training\nprocess for word embedding models is more complex. For example, there are multiple details in\nthis example that can be further optimized:\n• Data Preprocessing Stage: The data preprocessing stage ignores issues such as how\nto handle stop words and punctuation. Tokenization for large blocks of text is not\ndemonstrated.\n• Model Training Process: In the model training process, this example passes the index\nof a single word (via context_idx). However, when dealing with natural language","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":60,"pageEnd":61,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::53","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 2 29","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"processing tasks, especially when using embedding layers, it is common to process a\nbatch of data rather than a single data point. This approach can improve training efficiency\nand is also the data processing method expected by most deep learning frameworks. For\ndemonstration purposes, the example handles a single data point, but this is not best\npractice in real-world scenarios.\nThe model training process uses a simple SGD optimizer. In practical applications, more\nsophisticated optimizers such as Adam or RMSprop may be used. These optimizers\ntypically converge faster and can more effectively handle issues such as vanishing or\nexploding gradients.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":61,"pageEnd":61,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::54","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 2 31","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 31\nTypically, the training process also includes a validation step for evaluating the model’s\nperformance on unseen data, thereby monitoring and preventing overfitting. This part\nis not covered in this example.\nWord embeddings allow the Transformer model to represent the meaning of tokens as numerical\nvectors, but they do not by themselves tell the model where each token appears in a sequence.\nSince word order is essential for understanding language, the next section introduces positional\nencoding, which adds sequence-position information to the token representations.\nEncoding word order with positional information\nPositional encoding is a key design in the Transformer model that allows it to process sequence\norder without relying on recurrence or convolution. Without positional information, the\nTransformer would treat the input as a set of tokens and would not be able to distinguish where\neach token appears in a sentence. This could cause the model to misunderstand sentences where\nword order changes the meaning, such as “He fights the tiger” and “The tiger fights him.”\nPositional encoding addresses this problem by adding position information to the token\nembeddings before they are processed by the self-attention mechanism. As a result, the model\ncan use both the meaning of each token and its position in the sequence when learning sentence\nstructure and context.\nThe original Transformer paper, Attention Is All You Need, used sinusoidal positional encoding. In\nthis approach, the encoding for each position is generated using sine and cosine functions. For\neach position in the sequence, the positional encoding is defined as follows:\nFor even dimensions 2i:\nPE(pos, 2i) = sin(wi × pos)\nFor odd dimensions 2i + 1:\nPE(pos, 2i + 1) = cos(wi × pos)\nHere, 𝑤𝑤𝑖𝑖  is a frequency parameter related to dimension 𝑖𝑖 , usually set as 𝑤𝑤𝑖𝑖 = 1/10000\n2𝑖𝑖 / 𝑖𝑖 model ,\nwhere 𝑑𝑑 model  represents the model dimension.\nThe main advantage of sinusoidal positional encoding is that it can represent relative position\nrelationships through linear transformations. Because the encoding is generated from fixed\nmathematical functions rather than learned for a fixed maximum sequence length, it can be\napplied to sequences of different lengths. However, for very long sequences, the periodic nature\nof sine and cosine functions may make distant positions harder to distinguish, which can affect\nthe model’s ability to capture subtle long-range positional relationships.\nUnderstanding the Transformer Architecture Behind Large Language Models 32\nLater models, such as BERT and GPT-2, used learned absolute positional embeddings. In this\napproach, each position has a learned vector that is added to the token embedding. For example,\nBERT uses the sum of token embeddings, segment embeddings, and positional embeddings as","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":62,"pageEnd":63,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::55","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 2 31","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"its input representation, as shown in Figure 2.4.\nFigure 2.4: The concept of absolute positional encoding in the BERT model\nLearned absolute positional embeddings are straightforward to implement because each position\nis assigned a trainable vector. However, they are usually tied to a predefined maximum sequence\nlength. For example, BERT is commonly configured with a maximum input length of 512 tokens.\nIf a sequence exceeds the supported length, the model cannot directly use learned positional\nembeddings for the additional positions unless the architecture or embedding table is extended.\nTo address the limitations of fixed or absolute position representations, researchers have proposed\nrelative positional encoding methods. Instead of representing only the absolute position of each\ntoken, these methods model the relative distance between tokens. One influential approach is\nrelation-aware self-attention, where relative position representations are incorporated into the\nattention calculation so that the model can account for how far apart tokens are from one another.\nThe core advantage of relative position encoding lies in its ability to capture the relative positional\nrelationships between elements in a sequence through learned positional representations. This\nallows the model to better understand and distinguish the relative distances and directions\nbetween different positions. A major drawback of relative position encoding is that it depends\non specific task data, so it may not generalize well to other tasks. In addition, the computational\ncost of relative position encoding can be high, as it requires calculating the relative position\nbetween every pair of elements. This may decrease the efficiency of model training and inference.\nBuilding on the idea of relative position information, newer techniques such as Rotary Position\nEmbedding (RoPE) and Attention with Linear Biases (ALiBi) have been introduced. RoPE\nincorporates relative position information through rotation operations applied during attention\ncomputation, making it effective for handling longer sequences without adding many extra\nparameters. Models in the Llama family and Falcon use RoPE.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":63,"pageEnd":63,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::56","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 2 33","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 33\nALiBi introduces a linear bias into the attention scores so that the model can account for relative\ndistance during attention calculation. Instead of adding a separate positional embedding to the\ntoken representation, ALiBi modifies the attention scores directly. This helps the model handle\nlonger contexts more efficiently and allows positional information to influence attention behavior.\nBecause RoPE and ALiBi modify how positional information is incorporated into attention, they\nwill be revisited in the next section when the self-attention mechanism is introduced.\nOverall, positional encoding methods have evolved from fixed sinusoidal encodings to learned\nabsolute embeddings and relative-position techniques such as RoPE and ALiBi. These developments\nhave improved Transformer models in several ways:\n• Generalization: Positional encoding methods help models handle sequences of different\nlengths more effectively\n• Flexibility: Learned or relative position representations allow models to adapt positional\ninformation to specific tasks and architectures\n• Efficiency: Relative-position methods such as RoPE and ALiBi can improve long-sequence\nprocessing by incorporating position information directly into attention computation\n• Performance: Better positional representations help models capture long-distance\ndependencies and improve performance across NLP tasks\nPositional information tells the Transformer where each token appears in a sequence, but position\nalone is not enough for understanding language. The model must also determine which tokens\nare most relevant to one another in a given context. The next section introduces the attention\nmechanism, which allows the Transformer to calculate these relationships and assign greater\nimportance to the parts of the sequence that matter most for the current prediction.\nExplaining the attention mechanism\nThe attention mechanism is an extremely sophisticated data processing technique. It enables\nthe model, when dealing with a sequence, to not only focus on different positions within the\nsequence but also on the relationships between elements inside the sequence. Self-attention is\nan extension of the attention mechanism; it provides a more flexible and powerful way to handle\nsequential data, allowing the model to establish direct dependencies between any positions in\na sequence. The introduction of the self-attention mechanism, especially in the Transformer\nmodel, has become a milestone in the NLP field, greatly advancing the development of pretrained\nlanguage models.\nUnderstanding the Transformer Architecture Behind Large Language Models 34\nBelow, we will explain the core elements of the self-attention mechanism through a simple\nexample and then briefly introduce its implementation in the Transformer model.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":64,"pageEnd":65,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::57","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 2 33","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Suppose you encounter the following fill-in-the-blank question, as shown in Figure 2.5, which is\na typical conceptual diagram of the attention mechanism.\nBuy apples or buy ______\nFigure 2.5: Conceptual diagram of the attention mechanism for “apples”\nIf there is no further context, it is difficult to determine what should be filled in the blank. However,\nif the context is that the fruit at home has been eaten up, you can infer that the blank should\nbe filled with another kind of fruit, such as “oranges.” If the context is that your wife’s phone is\nbroken, then you can infer that the blank should be filled with Samsung or Xiaomi, because in\nthis case, “apple” refers to a phone.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":65,"pageEnd":65,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::58","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 2 35","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 35\nThrough the demonstration in Figure 2.5, we can understand attention as the association\nrelationship between keywords and context. Suppose the attention mechanism connects the\nblank with mobile phones, then the content to fill in becomes a matter of choosing between\ndifferent phone brands. If the attention mechanism links the blank with fruits, then the filled\ncontent is a matter of choosing between different fruits. The probabilities of different brands or\nfruits appearing are related to the sample data provided during training.\nThe attention mechanism first appeared in the paper Neural Machine Translation by Jointly Learning\nto Align and Translate. This paper proposed a new neural machine translation model that combines\nthe encoder-decoder architecture with the attention mechanism, enabling the model to focus\non the parts of the input sequence most relevant to the current output. The initially introduced\nattention mechanism was mainly used to weight the outputs of the encoder so that the decoder\ncould focus on the parts of the input sequence most relevant to the current generated word. This\nmechanism is generally used as a component of the encoder-decoder architecture.\nThe attention mechanism in the paper Neural Machine Translation by Jointly Learning to Align\nand Translate is divided into two parts: learning global correspondences and learning local\ncorrespondences.\nLearning global correspondences\nThe model uses a single attention mechanism to learn the global correspondences between the\nsource and target languages. Specifically, the model first encodes each word in the source and\ntarget languages using word embeddings to obtain word vectors. Then, the attention mechanism\nis used to compute the similarity between each target word and all source words. Finally, a\nweighted sum based on attention weights is performed on the source words to obtain a context\nvector relevant to the target word.\nLearning local correspondences\nThe model first uses the context vector from the first stage as a query vector, then uses the\nsource language word vectors as key vectors and value vectors, and finally calculates the local\ncorrespondences between each target word and all source words through the attention mechanism.\nThe attention mechanism was first used to help a decoder focus on relevant parts of an encoded\nsource sequence. In the Transformer model, this idea is extended through self-attention, where\nthe words within the same sequence compare with one another to determine which tokens are\nmost relevant in context.\nUnderstanding the Transformer Architecture Behind Large Language Models 36\nShowing how self-attention captures sentence context\nThe basic principle of the self-attention mechanism is the comparison of similarity between\nwords. In the attention mechanism, every word is compared for similarity with other words,","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":66,"pageEnd":67,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::59","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 2 35","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"and the degree of similarity determines the importance of each word within the sentence. This\nsimilarity comparison helps the model better understand semantic relationships in the sentence,\nand thus more accurately capture the semantic information of the sentence.\nIts specific principle can be explained in three parts: word embedding transformation, similarity\ncalculation, and spatial transformation.\nWord embedding transformation\nConverting textual information into vector representations is necessary for computers to\nunderstand text, since computers can only process numeric information. A good word embedding\nmodel enables computers to better comprehend the text and thus capture its semantic information\nmore effectively.\nSimilarity calculation\nCalculate the similarity between each word in a sentence and every other word. This step is aimed\nat finding the correlation between each word and every other word in the sentence.\nSpatial transformation\nApply different transformations to the word embedding vector data to find the most suitable\ntransformation method, so as to better capture the semantic relationships between words.\nNext, let’s use a simple example to illustrate this process. Suppose the following sentence is input:\nMy wife's phone is broken, should I buy an Apple or Android?\nFirst, the computer will extract the words from the text and convert them into vector representations.\nHigh-quality word embedding models can help computers better understand text and thus\ncapture semantic information more accurately. We use the pre-trained model bert-base-uncased\nand reduce the word embeddings to two dimensions using PCA, thereby visualizing the words\nin these two sentences. Figure 2.6 shows the visualization effect of the word embedding vectors.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":67,"pageEnd":67,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::60","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 2 37","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 37\nFigure 2.6: Visualization of word embedding vectors in 2D space\nWhen processing sequences (such as text), the attention mechanism allows the model to\ndynamically focus on certain parts of the sequence. This mechanism enables the model to\nidentify and assign higher weights to keywords in a sentence, thereby improving the efficiency\nand effectiveness of information processing. The processing flow is roughly divided into the\nfollowing four steps:\n1. Similarity calculation � The model calculates the similarity between each word in the\nsentence (called the “query”) and all other words (called the “keys”), usually achieved\nvia dot product or other correlation measures.\n2. Normalization � Next, the softmax function is used to normalize these similarity scores,\nso that the sum of all scores corresponding to each word equals 1. The purpose of this\nstep is to convert the raw similarity scores into a probability distribution, highlighting\nthe words in the sentence that are most relevant to the current word.\nUnderstanding the Transformer Architecture Behind Large Language Models 38\n3. Weighted sum � After normalized scoring for each word, the model computes a weighted\nsum of the “values” of all words in the sentence to generate a weighted representation.\nThis step focuses on the parts most relevant to the current word, reducing or ignoring\nthe influence of less relevant parts.\n4. Output � Finally, the model generates a weighted representation for each word in the\nsentence, capturing the interrelations between words and their relative importance to\nthe current word.\nWe performed similarity calculation on the above word embedding vectors, and the results of\nthe similarity calculation between words are shown in Figure 2.7.\nFigure 2.7: Similarity calculation results","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":68,"pageEnd":69,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::61","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 2 39","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 39\nThrough softmax normalization, we can obtain the weights of each word relative to other words.\nBy computing the “weighted sum,”, we realize an attention-based update of word correlations. A\ncomparison between word embedding and attention weight embedding is shown in Figure 2.8.\nFigure 2.8: Comparison of word embeddings and attention weight embeddings\nThe left side of Figure 2.8 shows the output of the original word embedding model, while the\nright side presents the weights computed through the attention mechanism. By comparison, we\ncan observe three changes after adding attention weights:\n• The position of “one phone broke” is tighter, making the grouping with other phrases\nmore distinct\n• The relationship between the words “buy” and “Android” is closer\n• The positional relationship between “Android” and “Apple” is closer, which can be judged\nby the value changes on the vertical axis\nThe above case is merely a simple simulation of attention weight calculation. In fact, within the\nTransformer model, the attention mechanism is implemented through Multi-Head Attention.\nMulti-head attention allows the model to focus on different parts of the input sequence\nsimultaneously, thereby improving the model’s expressiveness and generalization ability.\nUnderstanding the Transformer Architecture Behind Large Language Models 40\nThis conceptual example shows why attention is useful: it allows the model to assign different\nlevels of importance to different tokens based on context. The next section moves from this\nintuition to implementation by explaining how Transformer self-attention uses query, key, and\nvalue representations to calculate attention weights in practice.\nImplementing transformer self-attention\nIn the previous sections, the attention mechanism of the Transformer model was explained\nfrom a conceptual and theoretical perspective. However, the actual design and implementation\nof the model are more complex. This section will explain the practical workflow of the attention\nmechanism and the multi-head attention mechanism.\nThe attention mechanism addresses the following two problems.\nIt enables dynamic focus on the information most relevant to the current task, regardless of the\nposition of that information in the sequence. This is not limited to relationships within a sentence\nbut also includes long-range dependencies across sentences and even entire documents.\nThe attention mechanism dynamically assigns weights to each word in a sequence, allowing for\nmore effective use of long-distance contextual information and thus improving the accuracy of\nthe next word prediction.\nThe data processing workflow of the Transformer model includes the following four steps:\n1. At the input stage, the Transformer model converts text sequences into word embedding","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":70,"pageEnd":71,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::62","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 2 39","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"vectors, and supplements positional encoding information in the word embedding vectors\n(using sine and cosine encoding, respectively, for odd and even positions)\n2. Using word embeddings containing positional encoding information, the model generates\ncorresponding query (Q), key (K), and value (V) representations through the Q, K, and\nV weight matrices in the model. These representations are used to calculate attention\nweights, which reflect the correlation among different parts of the input sequence. By\ncombining the computed attention weights with the values, the final attention output is\ngenerated. This output captures the contextual information of the sequence and can be\nused for the next layer of the model.\n3. After the processing of each Transformer layer, the result calculated by the attention\nmechanism is directly added into the residual network. The data then passes through the\nfeed-forward network, goes through the residual connection and layer normalization, and\nis finally normalized to yield the probability distribution for the next word prediction result.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":71,"pageEnd":71,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf::63","sourceKey":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","sourceType":"pdf","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","filePath":"2 - Source Material/Books/llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","fileName":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops.pdf","folder":"2 - Source Material/Books","title":"llmsformodernsoftwaredeliveryanddevops","heading":"Chapter 2 41","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Chapter 2 41\n4. The predicted result is used as new input, and together with previous inputs, positional\nencoding is updated for further prediction processing. In this process, multi-head masked\nprocessing is involved. To ensure that the model does not “see” words that have not\nyet been predicted during training, a mask is used to cover these words, simulating the\ngeneration process during inference and ensuring that each prediction is made based\nonly on preceding words.\nThe implementation of positional encoding has been explained earlier. This section focuses on\nhow to train and learn with word embedding vectors containing positional encoding information.\nFirst, we start with the calculation of word similarity. The core of the attention mechanism is to\nidentify important information in a sentence, with these important associations obtained through\nword similarity calculations. The result of the similarity calculation is highly dependent on the\nword embedding model. In natural language processing, the same word may have completely\ndifferent meanings in different contexts. To enable the model to accurately judge the similarity\nof words based on context and determine their meanings in specific scenarios, we need to train\nauxiliary model parameters to dynamically adjust weights according to the context, construct\nupdated word embedding vectors, allow the model to capture subtle differences in context,\nand assign different interpretations to the same words while preserving the original semantic\ninformation.\nLet’s illustrate this process with a simple example.\nSuppose during training, the Transformer is given the input sentence: “one Apple phone.” The\nattention mechanism of the Transformer model will compare the similarity of each word in the\nsentence with other words and determine the importance of each word in the sentence based\non the similarity scores. Taking the similarity calculation between “Apple” and “phone” as an\nexample, we can show the necessity of training the Q and K weight matrices.\nAs mentioned earlier, in the simulated two-dimensional word vector space, “Apple” could be\nrelated both to “fruit” and to “phone.” The raw word embedding vectors cannot dynamically\nadjust similarity results, so for polysemous words, a single embedding model struggles to adapt\nto similarity judgments in different contexts.\nTherefore, given an embedding model, we need to find a way to dynamically adjust its vector space\nso that the similarity calculation results for the same word can differ under different contexts.\nFigure 2.9 well demonstrates the practical effect of the word embedding transformation matrix:\nby training via machine learning, more suitable transformation matrices are found to achieve\nbetter word embedding performance.\nUnderstanding the Transformer Architecture Behind Large Language Models 42","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":72,"pageEnd":73,"embeddingF32":""}]}